+++ b/pluggy/changelog/442.bugfix.rst
@@ -0,0 +1 @@
+A default value declared by a hookimpl for an argument no longer overrides the value passed by the hook call or the hookspec's default. The precedence is now call value, then hookspec default, then hookimpl default.
--- a/pluggy/docs/index.rst
+++ b/pluggy/docs/index.rst
@@ -639,6 +639,35 @@
     pm.hook.myhook(config=config, args=args)
     # New caller; hookimpls will get new_arg="get this".
     pm.hook.myhook(config=config, args=args, new_arg="get this")
+
+
+Hookimpl argument defaults
+^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
+A *hookimpl* may also declare a default value for an argument. Defaults are
+resolved with the following precedence:
+
+1. The value passed by the hook call.
+2. The hookspec's default value.
+3. The hookimpl's own default value.
+
+In particular, a hookimpl default never hides a value supplied by the call
+or by the hookspec. It is only used when neither provides the argument, for
+example a new hookimpl written against an older hookspec which lacks the
+argument. This applies to plain hookimpls, wrappers and old-style hookwrappers.
+
+.. code-block:: python
+
+    @hookimpl
+    def myhook(config, new_arg="impl default"):
+        return new_arg
+
+
+    # Spec has no new_arg and call omits it: "impl default".
+    # Call passes new_arg="get this": "get this".
+
+.. versionchanged:: 1.7.0
+    A hookimpl default previously always took precedence over the value passed
+    by the call.
 
 
